From: Manuel de Brito Fontes <aledbf@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 14:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] x86/ioapic: Skip the boot-time RTE accesses that change
 nothing

Before the first initcall a 64-bit boot goes through the IO-APIC
redirection table several times. enable_IR_x2apic() saves every entry,
masks those that are not masked and, when interrupt remapping was not
enabled, writes every saved entry back. enable_IO_APIC() then reads
every entry to look for the i8259's ExtINT pin, and clear_IO_APIC()
reads each again, masks it with every other bit cleared, and reads it a
third time to check its remote IRR. Each 32-bit register access is two
MMIO accesses, the index written to IOREGSEL and the data read or
written at IOWIN, so an entry costs four MMIO accesses to read or to
write. In a guest each MMIO access is a VM exit.

On a QEMU q35 guest without an IOMMU (24 pins, all reset masked) that
is 96 accesses for the save, 96 for the restore, 96 for the ExtINT
search and 288 for the clear: 576 exits, counted by guest RIP.

Remove the three passes that change nothing:

 - mask_ioapic_entries() returns whether it masked anything, and
   enable_IR_x2apic() writes the saved entries back only if it did.
   Nothing else writes an entry between the save and the restore, so
   when nothing was masked every entry already holds what was saved.
   When an entry was masked, the restore is unchanged.

 - enable_IO_APIC() reads each entry once and uses it for both the
   ExtINT search and the clear. Clearing a pin changes neither the mask
   nor the delivery mode of another, which is all the search looks at,
   and the first ExtINT pin is still the one recorded.

 - clear_IO_APIC_pin() returns after its first read when the entry
   already reads as what it leaves behind: masked, every other bit zero,
   no remote IRR and no delivery pending. Writing that value again and
   reading it back can change nothing. Any other entry, an SMI pin, or
   one whose reserved bits read nonzero, goes through the clear as
   before.

The entries are still read: nothing assumes their reset state, which a
kexec'd or crash kernel, or other firmware, does not leave. On the guest
above the boot makes 96 accesses for the save and 96 for the single
read in enable_IO_APIC(), 384 fewer exits (counted from the source;
IO-APIC accesses measured 589 -> 205 a boot). At ~6.7 us a nested exit
that is ~2.6 ms of a nested cold boot (inferred).

The suspend and resume paths are unchanged: resume still restores every
entry, since the IO-APIC lost them. Reboot, kexec and crash, which call
clear_IO_APIC(), skip the write and the check on an entry already
clear.

Measured on the bench fleet (nested KVM, Xeon 6975P-C, one vCPU, the
workspace's shape), alone this patch moved the first SSH login by less
than the noise. With the other four patches that remove exits from
device setup (the IO-APIC table, the state save at add, virtio's
capabilities, queue_select, the MSI message), the cold first login was
9.4 ms faster, 95% interval [5.7, 14.2], over 120 interleaved boots a
side (2026-11-23), and a restored guest's login did not move.

Local patch for this machine. Not upstream.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 arch/x86/include/asm/io_apic.h |  4 ++--
 arch/x86/kernel/apic/apic.c    | 10 ++++++--
 arch/x86/kernel/apic/io_apic.c | 43 +++++++++++++++++++++++++---------
 3 files changed, 42 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/x86/include/asm/io_apic.h b/arch/x86/include/asm/io_apic.h
index 0d806513c..56586e0d9 100644
--- a/arch/x86/include/asm/io_apic.h
+++ b/arch/x86/include/asm/io_apic.h
@@ -136,7 +136,7 @@ extern void ioapic_insert_resources(void);
 extern int arch_early_ioapic_init(void);
 
 extern int save_ioapic_entries(void);
-extern void mask_ioapic_entries(void);
+bool mask_ioapic_entries(void);
 extern int restore_ioapic_entries(void);
 
 extern void setup_ioapic_ids_from_mpc(void);
@@ -197,7 +197,7 @@ static inline int save_ioapic_entries(void)
 	return -ENOMEM;
 }
 
-static inline void mask_ioapic_entries(void) { }
+static inline bool mask_ioapic_entries(void) { return false; }
 static inline int restore_ioapic_entries(void)
 {
 	return -ENOMEM;
diff --git a/arch/x86/kernel/apic/apic.c b/arch/x86/kernel/apic/apic.c
index 90025451a..f867881e6 100644
--- a/arch/x86/kernel/apic/apic.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/apic/apic.c
@@ -1907,6 +1907,7 @@ void __init enable_IR_x2apic(void)
 {
 	unsigned long flags;
 	int ret, ir_stat;
+	bool masked;
 
 	if (ioapic_is_disabled) {
 		pr_info("Not enabling interrupt remapping due to skipped IO-APIC setup\n");
@@ -1925,7 +1926,7 @@ void __init enable_IR_x2apic(void)
 
 	local_irq_save(flags);
 	legacy_pic->mask_all();
-	mask_ioapic_entries();
+	masked = mask_ioapic_entries();
 
 	/* If irq_remapping_prepare() succeeded, try to enable it */
 	if (ir_stat >= 0)
@@ -1933,7 +1934,12 @@ void __init enable_IR_x2apic(void)
 	/* ir_stat contains the remap mode or an error code */
 	try_to_enable_x2apic(ir_stat);
 
-	if (ir_stat < 0)
+	/*
+	 * Only the mask has written an entry since the save. When it had
+	 * nothing to mask, every entry still holds what was saved, and
+	 * writing it back would only cost two accesses a register.
+	 */
+	if (ir_stat < 0 && masked)
 		restore_ioapic_entries();
 	legacy_pic->restore_mask();
 	local_irq_restore(flags);
diff --git a/arch/x86/kernel/apic/io_apic.c b/arch/x86/kernel/apic/io_apic.c
index 7d7175d01..4e7c3a1cc 100644
--- a/arch/x86/kernel/apic/io_apic.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/apic/io_apic.c
@@ -465,15 +465,24 @@ static void eoi_ioapic_pin(int vector, struct mp_chip_data *data)
 		__eoi_ioapic_pin(entry->apic, entry->pin, vector);
 }
 
-static void clear_IO_APIC_pin(unsigned int apic, unsigned int pin)
+static void clear_IO_APIC_entry(unsigned int apic, unsigned int pin,
+				struct IO_APIC_route_entry entry)
 {
-	struct IO_APIC_route_entry entry;
+	struct IO_APIC_route_entry cleared = { .masked = true };
 
 	/* Check delivery_mode to be sure we're not clearing an SMI pin */
-	entry = ioapic_read_entry(apic, pin);
 	if (entry.delivery_mode == APIC_DELIVERY_MODE_SMI)
 		return;
 
+	/*
+	 * An entry that already reads as what the clear leaves behind,
+	 * masked with no remote IRR and nothing pending, has nothing to
+	 * clear and nothing to check afterwards. A virtual IO-APIC resets
+	 * every entry to this, and there each access skipped is a VM exit.
+	 */
+	if (entry.w1 == cleared.w1 && entry.w2 == cleared.w2)
+		return;
+
 	/*
 	 * Make sure the entry is masked and re-read the contents to check
 	 * if it is a level triggered pin and if the remote-IRR is set.
@@ -509,6 +518,11 @@ static void clear_IO_APIC_pin(unsigned int apic, unsigned int pin)
 		       mpc_ioapic_id(apic), pin);
 }
 
+static void clear_IO_APIC_pin(unsigned int apic, unsigned int pin)
+{
+	clear_IO_APIC_entry(apic, pin, ioapic_read_entry(apic, pin));
+}
+
 void clear_IO_APIC (void)
 {
 	int apic, pin;
@@ -572,10 +586,11 @@ int save_ioapic_entries(void)
 }
 
 /*
- * Mask all IO APIC entries.
+ * Mask all IO APIC entries. Returns whether any entry had to be masked.
  */
-void mask_ioapic_entries(void)
+bool mask_ioapic_entries(void)
 {
+	bool masked = false;
 	int apic, pin;
 
 	for_each_ioapic(apic) {
@@ -589,9 +604,11 @@ void mask_ioapic_entries(void)
 			if (!entry.masked) {
 				entry.masked = true;
 				ioapic_write_entry(apic, pin, entry);
+				masked = true;
 			}
 		}
 	}
+	return masked;
 }
 
 /*
@@ -1279,19 +1296,26 @@ void __init enable_IO_APIC(void)
 	if (!nr_legacy_irqs() || !nr_ioapics)
 		return;
 
+	/*
+	 * Read each pin once, both to find the pin in ExtINT mode and to
+	 * clear it: do not trust the IO-APIC being empty at bootup. Clearing
+	 * a pin changes neither the mask nor the delivery mode of another,
+	 * which is all the search looks at.
+	 */
 	for_each_ioapic_pin(apic, pin) {
-		/* See if any of the pins is in ExtINT mode */
 		struct IO_APIC_route_entry entry = ioapic_read_entry(apic, pin);
 
 		/*
 		 * If the interrupt line is enabled and in ExtInt mode I
 		 * have found the pin where the i8259 is connected.
 		 */
-		if (!entry.masked && entry.delivery_mode == APIC_DELIVERY_MODE_EXTINT) {
+		if (ioapic_i8259.pin == -1 && !entry.masked &&
+		    entry.delivery_mode == APIC_DELIVERY_MODE_EXTINT) {
 			ioapic_i8259.apic = apic;
 			ioapic_i8259.pin  = pin;
-			break;
 		}
+
+		clear_IO_APIC_entry(apic, pin, entry);
 	}
 
 	/*
@@ -1313,9 +1337,6 @@ void __init enable_IO_APIC(void)
 	if (((ioapic_i8259.apic != i8259_apic) || (ioapic_i8259.pin != i8259_pin)) &&
 	    (i8259_pin >= 0) && (ioapic_i8259.pin >= 0))
 		pr_warn("ExtINT in hardware and MP table differ\n");
-
-	/* Do not trust the IO-APIC being empty at bootup */
-	clear_IO_APIC();
 }
 
 void native_restore_boot_irq_mode(void)
-- 
2.53.0

